+    Trace the inside rim of a sealed chamber, break its thinnest wall, and extend a pipe around the outgoing beam.
+
+    Input structure:
+    - The dominant color is background. The most common non-background color forms one closed rectangular shell around a cavity.
+    - A smaller non-wall color seed lies inside that cavity near the side opposite the eventual exit. Exactly one wall side is 1 cell thick; the other three sides are thicker. Any remaining non-background pixels are sparse obstacles outside the shell.
+
+    Rule:
+    - Recover the enclosed chamber from the seed, compare the four wall-side thicknesses, and choose the uniquely thinnest side as the breach direction.
+    - Inside the chamber, color only the reachable background cells that touch the wall, leaving the interior core hollow. Replace the thin wall strip with fill, extend a straight fill beam outward from that breach, and recolor the flanking extension bands as wall so the beam sits inside a pipe. Obstacles stay fixed and cast background shadows only through the external beam.
